WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.385 --> 00:00:15.560
We'll just start out
with this basic thing where

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:30.520
I can use file inputs to
upload anything that I'd like,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.500
which is cool.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.820
But this whole
request was asking

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.880
if we could add a
capture attributes

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.320
to fileinputs to enable--
what was it again Barret

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.505
to basically enable the
front facing camera?

00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:52.390
Yeah, to enable the
camera, the input.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.140
There is already an excepts
argument to file input.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.010
There was not the
capture attribute.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.450
Right.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:02.950
Right.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:10.620
So fileinput has
handful of arguments

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:16.770
and accept allows us to
upload various different types

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.290
of files, is that?

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.960
So we could say accept
is image slash star,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.150
so that you can
only upload images.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.850
You wouldn't be able to upload
a CSV or something like that.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.610
I see.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:39.060
I say except video
image slash star.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.140
Now if I run this
again, you can do your--

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.660
I can upload my P&amp;amp;Gs but I
can't upload anything else,

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.600
you know, like PDFs.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.245
Cool.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.470
OK.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:55.160
Right.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:01.160
So and if we actually look at
the return value of fileinput,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:07.350
it's a pretty big complicated
HTML tag that's being returned.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:13.820
So there's like an outer div,
which is the actual Shiny input

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.590
container that
you'll see around.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.800
A lot of Shiny input functions.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.070
And that's just kind of
a class to let the Shiny

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.950
JavaScript know like hey,
this is an input container

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:29.480
that has input value that we
should send back to the server,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.650
that you could access
in the server code.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.670
But there's all this sort
of complicated markup.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.670
And then finally, at
the end of the day,

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:48.830
there's an input tag that is
the actual HTML element that

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.370
enables the file uploading.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:57.170
So essentially, we have this
accept argument on file input

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:02.150
that gets routed to this accept
attribute, the input tag.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.670
So we have this kind
of general problem

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:12.090
with a lot of Shiny
input functions,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.120
where we're basically
wrapping HTML tags.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.900
And in some cases,
you might want

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:27.750
to customize certain
attributes on particular tags

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.940
on this return value.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.630
And we might not sort
of out of the box,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:37.365
provide you the tools
to be able to customize,

00:03:37.365 --> 00:03:41.010
like a very particular attribute
on a very particular tag

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:43.510
in this return value.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:48.360
So this person was kind of
requesting, like hey, there's

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:55.290
this capture attribute on
and HTML input tag that

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.790
allows you to enable
the front facing camera

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:00.850
on a mobile phone.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:04.200
So this would allow him to kind
of do like a facial recognition

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:11.370
thing or allow somebody to
capture a video of themselves

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.830
in then upload it
to a Shiny map.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:22.110
And we've been working hard
on tools to basically allow

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:26.430
you to more generally take
the return value of something

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.650
like file input and target
a very particular say

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:37.830
HTML elements like the input
element within this return

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.930
structure, and do something
like append an attribute, which

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:47.970
is effectively what this
user is asking for is hey,

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.400
could you add an
argument to file input

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.550
to basically
customize the capture

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.840
attribute on this input tag.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.820
So we're trying to
avoid going down

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.570
this sort of rabbit hole of
trying to cover every HTML

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:11.370
attribute on every sort of Shiny
UI function you could imagine,

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:15.900
and providing better tools for
doing this sort of thing, where

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.340
this user could instead of
just having the file input,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:30.740
here basically, do something
like this where instead

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:31.970
of having--

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.800
this is the return
value where I have

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:37.970
this input where it's a
type text plus form control

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.130
placeholder read only--

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.710
And input above it, as well.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:46.230
Yes, this is the actual
input that's relevant.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.320
Good.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.190
Pointing that out.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:54.170
We can use this tag
append attributes

00:05:54.170 --> 00:06:02.690
which effectively does some
CSS look magic to say--

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:06.080
within this HTML
structure that's

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.000
being returned by this
first argument here,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.070
find the first input tag.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:16.690
So it'll go in and find this
input tag here, and append

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.540
an attribute of capture user.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.656
So that's adding this part here.

00:06:22.656 --> 00:06:25.070
You could even do--

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.340
because it selected
actually both input tags

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.250
because there's no
distinguishing marker,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.600
we could do id foo on the CSS.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:35.170
Right.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:35.730
Right.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:36.230
Right.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.770
The CSS selector-- this--

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.990
when I just type out
input, it's basically

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.400
going to look for a tag
name of inputs, that's just

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.910
kind of how CSS selectors work.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.100
But there is some
special characters

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.770
that you can add
to CSS selectors,

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:03.710
where I could tag on a
hashtag or pound sign.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.260
And then an id name.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:12.410
So it's very pointed out,
I could do pound sign who.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.870
And that would make
sure that instead

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:19.420
of the selector matching
both input tags,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:22.660
notice how the capture
users only being

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.500
added to this input with an id
of foo is instead of this one,

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:27.230
as well.

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:29.770
So, yeah with tag
append attributes,

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.770
this is actually a
fairly recent feature

00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:36.730
that we've added to
tag append attributes.

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:41.728
In the past, you've
had two kind of--

00:07:41.728 --> 00:07:43.870
maybe some of you have
already done something

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:51.550
like this, where if
you actually look

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:56.020
at the structure like the R
representation of this HTML,

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:00.220
it's a big mess of
complicated list structures

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:01.930
that you get back.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:04.090
And there's a very good
reason why we do this.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:11.020
It's by having like an actual
formal structure to these HTML

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.740
tags, it allows us to compose
them in interesting ways,

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:19.000
and do sort of magical things
like this tagAppendAttributes.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:23.290
But this is not something you
want to work directly with.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:26.350
So like, way early
on in HTML tools

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:31.330
you might have had to
do something like $x.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.559
Let me reach into the children
of this top level div tag,

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:38.110
and then look at
what's in there.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:44.610
It's a list of three, where
the first list is a label,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.780
the second list is a div
that holds the input tag.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:55.160
So let me actually reach
into the second's child's.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.430
And I actually want to get
into the children of this div,

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.155
so now I'd have to do this.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.530
And now I'm getting closer to
getting to this actual input

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:14.070
that I want, but now I need
to reach into this first item,

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:15.430
get into the children.

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.190
So as you can see
here, this is not

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.760
something you want to actually--

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.680
part of what inspired
some of this work

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.760
as we've seen a lot of code
out in the ecosystem doing

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.850
this kind of stuff
to do custom UI work.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:34.620
And we wanted to make
it a lot less confusing.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.340
How to do something like this.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.230
Also make your code a lot less
brittle to breaking changes

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.140
that we make to kind
of the HTML that we're

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.190
returning in these functions.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.720
So I've had to go out and
actually fix cases like this,

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.080
because we want to just
slightly tweak the actual HTML

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.730
that we're returning.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:59.520
Or this is like making
very strong assumptions

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.995
about the relative
ordering of these tags.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.330
And working with an API,
that's like not super public

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.310
and something we don't
really want to encourage

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.150
you to actually be doing.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.150
So it's a lot better
to like actually

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:20.400
reach in and get that subset
of HTML that you're looking for

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.165
and modify it using
something like this.

00:10:23.165 --> 00:10:27.150
Yeah, like placing a
needle within a haystack

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:30.270
in a very specific
location versus rummaging

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:34.026
through and going eh, I
hop, and then leaving it.

00:10:34.026 --> 00:10:39.690
And so actually what
powers this CSS selector

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.830
argument and the ability
to kind of subset

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.550
down, find a particular
subset of this HTML

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.170
and then modify it, is
powered underneath the hood

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:56.160
by sort of a lower
level tool that you can,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:59.190
if you're kind of a power
user and need to do something

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:02.220
a little bit more
sophisticated than something

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.200
like tagAppendAttributes
would provide.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:14.800
We also have this
API called tagQuery.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.860
And if you've ever used
jQuery in JavaScript,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:25.130
this API will look very
similar to what you already

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.590
do with jQuery.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:30.980
So we're not really
inventing a new wheel here,

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:32.600
we're just kind
of borrowing some

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.840
of the best ideas
of what makes jQuery

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:38.810
or what made jQuery
such a great project.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.570
And you can find
this article here

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:47.690
at rstudio.github.io/htmltools.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:49.400
This is a fairly new
website, and we only

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.570
have one article as of now
that gets into tagQuery,

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:58.340
but hopefully we'll get some
more documentation surrounding

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.680
HTML tools.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:04.310
And if you're kind of confused,
like if you haven't really

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:09.830
heard of HTML tools
before, HTML tools provides

00:12:09.830 --> 00:12:15.050
kind of the HTML
foundation for Shiny.

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:20.720
So when something
like fileinput returns

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:24.470
all of this HTML
structuring all of that,

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.920
all of those data structures
and that writing of HTML logic

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:32.240
is all done by each, this
sort of lower level HTML tools

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.940
package.

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:36.890
And you might have never
had to do something

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:39.710
like library HTML tools
before, even if you've

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:44.720
written custom HTML because
Shiny re-export functions

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:46.310
from this package.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:49.370
So you might have written
like library Shiny

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:54.080
and then like create a
custom, HTML div or something

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.730
like that, because you get those
functions when you load Shiny.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:02.210
But if you load
HTML tools, you'll

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:07.640
get some other functions like
tagQuery, tagAppendAttributes.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.730
Some other functions for
doing like lower level

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:15.980
HTML tool thing or HTML
things that not a whole lot

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:18.080
of Shiny apps will be doing.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:24.300
So with tagQuery,
the main idea is

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:28.860
that you can pass it either
a tag object, like a div,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:34.860
or equivalently something like
the return value of our inputs.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:37.440
I could pass directly
into tagQuery.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:50.680
And there's two main components
to this return structure.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:56.260
One is this-- it's actually
a class with methods.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.640
So this is showing you.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:01.750
If I called the all
tags class, I basically

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:07.630
get the return value that
I fed into this tagQuery.

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:11.680
So it might not be clear why
it's set up this way right now,

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:16.560
but eventually we're going
to like modify the input

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:23.060
value essentially in
this tagQuery object.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:27.680
And then the other method is
this selected tags method,

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:31.970
which will allow
me to do something

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:40.090
like if I want to find
all the input tags,

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:42.990
essentially what we had
before with CSS selector

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:47.460
equal to input, the equivalent
way of doing this with tagQuery

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.560
is to say dollar find input.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.510
It's not going to update
the all tags output

00:14:55.510 --> 00:15:00.730
of this tagQuery object,
but it will essentially

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:06.450
subset down the selection
from being by default

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:09.500
the input that I gave it.

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:13.665
It will update the selectedTags
to both of the input tags.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:23.470
So, now I could do
something like change

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.170
a call to selectedTags.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:28.810
And now I have a
list of like two

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.780
containing both
of the input tags

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.720
that I might want to
do something with.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.810
And I don't think I can
just kind of on a whim

00:15:37.810 --> 00:15:43.450
explain better what tagQuery
where actually provides

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.430
than sort of this vignette.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:50.380
So I do recommend
reading through this.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.450
But kind of the
first portion of this

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:57.430
is all about this
like fine method

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.130
and similar methods
like children.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:03.400
Where fine will find, you
know, traverse essentially,

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:06.640
all of the descendants
of the top level tag,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:11.680
and find all
occurrences of HTML tags

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:17.780
that match the CSS selector
that you've given it.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:20.890
So that's why I was
able to use find here

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.590
and it traverses everything,
and it reaches in

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:30.560
to like several descendants
down into this input tag here.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.730
But if you wanted to do
something like for some reason,

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.350
only consider the
direct children,

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:44.170
which would be this label
div and this other div.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:47.200
If I search for
children, there's

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:50.500
going to be no selection
associated with that.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.940
That matches the input, right?

00:16:54.940 --> 00:17:02.130
But if I wanted to
find a label tag,

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:06.650
there is a direct
child label tag here.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:10.710
So that will match
on this label.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.770
So in the case of an
action button like,

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:18.260
it's a simple like
one single button tag

00:17:18.260 --> 00:17:25.609
which allows us to kind of
design this function interface

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:28.880
as routing all additional
arguments that you might want

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:32.300
to provide to this button tag.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:36.710
So that allows you to do
things like, my action

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:41.720
button with an idea
of who the label here.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:44.720
And that creates
this button HTML

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:48.590
with an id, the type,
and some default classes

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:52.370
to give its like default
bootstrap styling.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:57.260
But say I want to add like a
custom inline style of like I

00:17:57.260 --> 00:18:03.440
want to increase the font size
to basically two units of line

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:08.360
height, you can do something
like that with action button

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:17.100
because it basically is like
doing the lower level tags

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:25.320
dollar button, and providing a
style to that directly since we

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:28.530
sort of take dot, dot,
dot, and route it directly

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.240
to the tags button.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:34.960
It's essentially
the same interface.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.540
So in the case of
action button, it's

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.670
a very thin wrapper
where you don't really

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:46.140
need this sort of ability
to take the return value

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.120
and then modify attributes.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.720
You can just do that
in one shot directly

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.085
when calling action button.

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:57.985
And you can send to this--

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:05.610
again is just kind of taking
any sort of input value

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:08.400
and feeding it into button.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:13.830
If I wanted to, I could pass
another tag object into that,

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:18.755
and like I don't create
just an empty div.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:27.040
Well, actually that's-- or icon.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:33.360
I think I'd have to
explicitly say icon is no.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:34.620
What is that?

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:35.120
Width.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.050
So that is kind
of an unfortunate

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:48.600
thing about the
way this is set up

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:51.690
as you would have to make sure
like, what was happening here

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.750
is this tag scalar div
instead of going into the dot,

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:59.230
dot, dot here, since I
hadn't specified an icon.

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:03.420
It was treating it like
the third argument here.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:07.200
But if I wanted to
take a tag object

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:10.440
and have it run through
this dot, dot, dot.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.650
I could fully specify
those and make sure

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:15.930
that it's going to the
dot, dot, dot there.

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:19.320
And it shows up in my HTML.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:22.710
Again like the tagQuery,
append attributes,

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:26.700
the CSS selector stuff
isn't super useful for this,

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:28.620
but it's more useful
in a case where

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:36.050
you have like a lot of like big
complicated HTML tag structure

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:39.380
and you need to get at a
particular subset of that.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.280
We did basically just
cover with the tag,

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:44.480
and tagAppendAttributes.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:49.700
So you have the ability to find
subsets with this CSS selector.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:54.230
And then ability to
append attributes.

00:20:54.230 --> 00:21:01.170
But there's more that you can do
than just appending attributes.

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:03.920
You can also do things
like append children.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:16.670
So and you'll notice this
other append function

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:19.490
also has a CSS
selector argument.

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:22.385
So you could do a
similar thing where--

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:26.960
again, here's my fileinput.

00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:34.400
If I wanted to say insert
another div within this input

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:45.110
group, I could search for
a class of input group

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:52.920
and append a child
tag just a div.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:58.590
And I'll just give it a
class of my custom class.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:05.330
And I should load--

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:08.750
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:09.920
Oh, thank you.

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:23.160
And now I actually
get this div of class,

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:27.180
my custom class
inserted by default

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:36.100
I guess in as like the last
child within this input group

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:36.730
tag.

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:38.590
So kind of like,
there's basically

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:41.890
two main ways you might
want to modify the HTML.

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:46.150
Either like mess with
the attributes on the tag

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:51.320
or add or remove tags.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:57.980
So if it's as simple as wanting
to like add an attribute,

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:03.720
or modify an
attribute, or append

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.800
a single child
like this, you can

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:08.690
use these sort of
higher level tag

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:13.220
append child or
append attributes.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:16.970
But as some of these
examples get into-- you

00:23:16.970 --> 00:23:21.965
can use this tagQuery to do
more sophisticated stuff.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:31.926
And let me try to
find an example of--

00:23:31.926 --> 00:23:37.760
You can do like
remove class and class

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:43.190
with like button success
instead of button default.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:45.950
Right.

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:59.805
Yeah, so let's try out tagQuery
with this action button.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:07.170
So by default, this button
tag is going to be selected.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:08.540
And as Barret was saying--

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:15.460
let me actually save this.

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:18.641
An object called BTN.

00:24:18.641 --> 00:24:20.630
And then when I
type dollar, I'll

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:26.330
see all of the available
methods on this tagQuery class.

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:32.370
So we've already seen stuff
like find and children.

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:34.730
There's also siblings
parent, and parents,

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:38.360
and this kind of helps you
traverse that tree, where say,

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:40.760
you went to a particular
subset but then

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:47.480
you actually want to go back to
a parent or find a sibling tag.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:51.140
You can use those methods.

00:24:51.140 --> 00:24:52.580
But he was mentioning--

00:24:56.060 --> 00:25:02.230
maybe I want to for some reason
take off this BTN default

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:12.780
class, I could do something like
remove this BTN default class.

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:20.180
And this will actually
modify the BTN object.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.140
And we do that for
performance reasons.

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:27.110
That's why this is kind of
like-- even like a lower level

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:29.240
developer, kind
of more developer

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:36.830
facing tool than this API where
it's not modifying the input

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:41.690
object here, it's giving you
basically a copy of that input

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:45.950
value as its return value.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:49.040
Whereas using tagQuery
more directly like this

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:53.390
actually will modify the
input tax that you give it.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:01.400
So, yeah, So now I've
removed BTN defaults

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:12.520
and now I could add a
class of say BTN success.

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:15.070
And now it's appended
to the end here.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:25.110
And maybe to make this a
little bit more motivating,

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:27.450
I'll just show you what
this, something like this

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:28.365
would actually do.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:38.090
Where that-- it's interesting.

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:41.090
I thought by default
actually it was going

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:49.760
to come up as a blue color.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:54.710
But that is only going to
come up as a blue color

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:59.930
if I add a class of BTN primary.

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:02.570
And that is a Bootstrap
specific thing

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:05.480
where again this is kind
of like a hard to discover

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:13.520
sort of feature where
Shiny comes with bootstrap,

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:17.420
and Bootstrap has these
sort of semantic CSS classes

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:23.360
where this BTN and BTN default
like gives that default

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:27.410
sort of white coloring
with a border around

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:30.050
it based on bootstraps defaults.

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:34.910
But if you add an additional
BTN primary class, that

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:40.250
will give it this sort of blue
background and border color

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:41.840
that makes it kind of pop out.

00:27:45.550 --> 00:27:48.180
So once you kind of aware
of those kind of things

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:51.390
and like more familiar
with how bootstrap works,

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:57.930
or if you're using some
other framework with Shiny,

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.580
knowing about these
kind of tricks

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:04.320
to like add or remove
classes from the return value

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:08.710
will help you kind of
be able to leverage

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:13.420
kind of the scaffolding
or the high level

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:16.510
like widgets that
Shiny provides to you,

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:19.570
but also be able to do like
some more custom theming, things

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:21.270
with it.

